Superficial abrasion from fall on synthetic turf (community sports) to lateral thigh
Minor Trauma and Personal Injury
Broad shallow epidermal loss, Intense superficial burning, Synthetic turf slide mechanism

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: MTI-029
Reason for Visit: Acute, large-surface-area painful friction burn requiring assessment for deep tissue damage and specialized barrier dressing application.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage identified a large surface area "turf burn" causing intense, unmanageable neuropathic pain due to exposed nerve endings. Urgent clinical evaluation was necessary to exclude deep fascial tears, thoroughly decontaminate the wound of synthetic turf fibers, and apply immediate soothing occlusive barrier dressings before daytime clinical services opened.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: I slid hard on the artificial turf and took a huge layer of skin off my thigh; the burning pain is unbearable.
History of Present Illness: Injury occurred 1 hour ago during a rapid sliding mechanism directly onto artificial sports grass. The patient reports a broad, linear area of superficial epidermal loss over the lateral thigh. The raw, erythematous surface remains highly sensitive to light physical touch and ambient air currents. There is mild serous weeping producing a glistening surface, without brisk or pulsatile active bleeding. The patient complains of intense superficial burning pain. There is minimal surrounding ecchymosis or deep muscular tissue swelling.
Functional Status: Ambulating with a limp to prevent clothing from brushing against the lateral thigh.
Social/Safety Concerns: Minor restriction in comfortable sleeping positions due to lateral thigh pain.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 128/84 | HR: 82 | Temp: 36.8 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 78 Kg
General: Alert, standing in the room to avoid sitting on the affected thigh, visible grimacing when clothing shifts.
Physical Exam: Dermatological assessment reveals a broad, shallow epidermal loss measuring 15x10cm over the lateral thigh. There is intense superficial burning pain elicited by ambient air flow. Complete absence of full-thickness skin loss or visibility of subcutaneous fat. The wound bed shows minimal exogenous synthetic fiber contamination. Normal underlying muscular function allows full, painless active range of motion of the hip and knee. There is minimal localized warmth without any signs of spreading infectious cellulitis, purulence, or fluctuance in the acute phase. Distal neurovascular exam is normal.
Environmental Observations: Sports uniform visibly torn at the thigh.
Medication Reconciliation: No routine medications.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Superficial abrasion (turf burn) to lateral thigh - Status: Stable
Differentials:

Deep partial-thickness thermal friction burn - Ruled out due to: Absence of blister formation, leathery eschar, and presence of immediate sharp pain indicating intact superficial nerve endings.

Traumatic fascial tear (muscle herniation) - Ruled out due to: Intact muscular contour beneath the abrasion and normal, pain-free active hip/knee range of motion against resistance.

Cellulitis - Ruled out due to: Immediate acute traumatic mechanism with erythema strictly confined to the zone of direct mechanical friction, lacking systemic signs.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

Extensive friction abrasions from synthetic surfaces (turf burns) present primarily as a severe pain management issue combined with a high risk of localized Staphylococcal infection from community sports environments. Bedside clinical assessment confidently excluded deep structural muscle injury and confirmed an isolated, broad epidermal defect. Urgent after-hours visitation was essential to perform immediate, rigorous saline irrigation to remove synthetic pathogens and to rapidly apply large-format occlusive hydrocolloid dressings, instantly terminating the severe, air-induced pain cycle that could not be managed safely at home overnight.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Wound irrigated copiously with normal saline. Large-format hydrocolloid/silicone adhesive dressings applied to completely cover the denuded area. Prescribed scheduled paracetamol and ibuprofen. Tetanus prophylaxis verified.
Positive Behavioral Change: Keep the large dressing completely sealed and dry for 5 days. By not disturbing the specialized dressing, you prevent the newly forming, fragile skin cells from being repeatedly ripped off, ensuring the fastest possible healing with the least amount of permanent scarring.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): Large-format hydrocolloid dressings.
Follow-up: Routine GP or practice nurse review in 5 days for a scheduled dressing change and assessment of epithelialization.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Present to the local doctor or Emergency Department if the wound begins leaking thick, green, foul-smelling fluid, if expanding red streaks travel up the thigh, or if you develop a fever and chills.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Turf burns are highly susceptible to secondary bacterial infections; strict hygiene around the dressing edges is mandatory.
Clinical Course: The intense stinging will completely resolve within 12 hours once the dressing seals. The dressing will bubble and turn white as it absorbs fluid. Full healing of this large area will take 2 to 3 weeks.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek medical attention immediately if the skin surrounding the bandage becomes rapidly swollen, hot to the touch, and increasingly painful.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Artificial turf acts like sandpaper, removing the top layer of skin and exposing all the raw nerve endings to the air. The dressing creates a sealed, moist environment that instantly blocks the air and eliminates the pain.
Actionable Steps: Do not peel the dressing back to check the wound. Wear loose clothing over the area. Shower carefully, avoiding direct water pressure on the dressing edges.

